\begin_layout Title
Adaptation and Vertical Control in the Airline Industry
\end_layout

\begin_layout Abstract
Silke Forbes and Mara Lederman: American Economic Review (2009).
 Give a brief history of U.S.
 airline industry.
 How did we get to hub and spoke?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Simplified 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Holdup Problem
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In this model, we have an upstream firm and a downstream firm, which we
 call 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

seller
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 S and 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

buyer
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 B, respectively.
 For example, the upstream firm is a coal mine, while the downstream firm
 is a power plant.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Now, B and S have a relationshp-specific investment, and the investment
 is not contractible.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The timing of the game is as follows:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the first stage of the game, B and S meet.
 Then, in the second stage, the seller invests e, and the buyer invests
 i.
 At the final stage, 
\begin_inset Formula $q=\{0,1\}$
\end_inset

 (trade or no trade), and 
\begin_inset Formula $t=\mbox{transfer from B to S}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Payoffs
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For the buyer:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $q\cdot R(i)-t-i$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 is the cost of investment and 
\begin_inset Formula $R(i)$
\end_inset

 is the rate of tunring 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset

 to money.
 We assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{\prime}>0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{\prime\prime}<0$
\end_inset

, so there is a decreasing marginal return of investment
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For the seller:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $-q\cdot C(e)+t-e$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $e$
\end_inset

 is the cost of investment and 
\begin_inset Formula $C(e)$
\end_inset

 denotes the production costs.
 We assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $C^{\prime}<0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $C^{\prime\prime}>0$
\end_inset

.
 So, there is diminishing returns to cost reduction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Case 1: First Best
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula ${\displaystyle \max_{i,e}}\ q\cdot R(i)-t-i-q\cdot C(e)+t-e$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $q[R(i)-C(e)]-i-e$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
F.O.C.: 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{\prime}(i)=1$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $C^{\prime}(e)=1$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Case 2: No ex-ante contracting
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
After investment, can observe 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $e$
\end_inset

 and contract on it.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
After investment, contract and neogtiate on surplus.
 (This comes right after the second stage of the extensive form game described
 above).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Equal split of surplus}=\frac{1}{2}\cdot q\cdot\left[R(i)-C(e)\right]$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
B and S invest
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For the buyer:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula ${\displaystyle \max_{i}}\ \frac{1}{2}\left[R(i)-C(e)\right]-i$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
F.O.C.: 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{\prime}(i)=2$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For the seller:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula ${\displaystyle \max_{e}}\ \frac{1}{2}\left[R(i)-C(e)\right]-e$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
F.O.C.: 
\begin_inset Formula $C^{\prime}(e)=-2$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Both the buyer and seller are under-investing.
 Because the buyer and seller are splitting the surplus, they are not obtaining
 the full marginal return of their investment.
 This is known as the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

hold up problem,
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 which is the primary reason why there is vertical integration between the
 buyer and seller.
\end_layout

